Foxconn's statement confirms that some of its North American factories suffered a cyberattack and are resuming normal production; it does not name sites, a start time or how long production stopped, and Foxconn declined to say how many factories were hit or to confirm that customer data was taken ([The Record, 2026-05-12](https://therecord.media/foxconn-confirms-cyberattack-north-american-factories); [The Register, 2026-05-12](https://www.theregister.com/cyber-crime/2026/05/12/foxconn-confirms-cyberattack-after-nitrogen-claims-apple-nvidia-data-theft/5239144)). Wisconsin, Ohio, Texas, Virginia, Indiana and Mexico are where Foxconn has factories, not a list of affected sites; the Mount Pleasant outage from 2026-05-01 comes from workers quoted by The Cybersec Guru, and Houston from AppleInsider ([9to5Mac, 2026-05-12](https://9to5mac.com/2026/05/12/apple-supplier-foxconn-confirms-ransomware-attack-affected-north-american-factories/)). Coveware's finding concerns Nitrogen's ESXi encryptor, whose bug leaves files unrecoverable even for the attackers, and its advice is to analyse encrypted files against the specific malware sample before assuming they can or cannot be recovered ([Coveware, 2026-02-02](https://www.coveware.com/blog/2026/2/2/nitrogen-ransomware-esxi-malware-has-a-bug)).
